.TH PRINTF 3 "July 2025" "Holberton-School Printf" "User Manual"
.SH NAME
_printf \- formatted output conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.BI "int _printf(const char *" format ", ...);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf
function produces output according to a
.I format
specifier. It writes output to stdout, the standard output stream. The
.I format
string is composed of zero or more directives: ordinary characters (not
.B %
), which are copied unchanged to the output stream, and conversion specifications, each of which results in fetching zero or more subsequent arguments.
.PP
Conversion specifiers are introduced by the character
.B %
and end with a conversion specifier. Between them, there may be (in order) zero or more flags, an optional minimum field width, an optional precision, and an optional length modifier. This implementation supports the following conversion specifiers:
.TP
.B %c
Single character (char)
.TP
.B %s
String of characters (char *)
.TP
.B %d, %i
Signed decimal integer (int)
.TP
.B %u
Unsigned decimal integer (unsigned int)
.TP
.B %o
Unsigned octal (unsigned int)
.TP
.B %x
Unsigned hexadecimal integer (lowercase) (unsigned int)
.TP
.B %X
Unsigned hexadecimal integer (uppercase) (unsigned int)
.TP
.B %p
Pointer address (void *)
.TP
.B %%
A single '%' character
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, the total number of characters written is returned. On failure, -1 is returned.
.SH CONVERSION SPECIFIERS
After the initial
.BR % ,
the following conversions are supported:
.TP
.B c
The
.I int
argument is converted to an
.I unsigned char
, and the resulting character is written.
.TP
.B s
The
.I char *
argument is expected to be a pointer to a character array (string). Characters from the array are written up to (but not including) a terminating null byte ('\0'). If the pointer is NULL, the string "(null)" is printed.
.TP
.B d, i
The
.I int
argument is converted to signed decimal notation. Negative values are preceded with a '-' sign.
.TP
.B u
The
.I unsigned int
argument is converted to unsigned decimal notation.
.TP
.B o
The
.I unsigned int
argument is converted to unsigned octal notation.
.TP
.B x
The
.I unsigned int
argument is converted to unsigned hexadecimal notation using lowercase letters (a-f).
.TP
.B X
The
.I unsigned int
argument is converted to unsigned hexadecimal notation using uppercase letters (A-F).
.TP
.B p
The
.I void *
pointer argument is printed in hexadecimal form (as if by %#x) with a leading "0x" prefix.
.TP
.B %
A '%' is written. No argument is converted.
.SH EXAMPLES
To print a string and integer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "main.h"

int main(void)
{
    _printf("Hello %s, your score is %d\\n", "Alice", 95);
    return (0);
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
Hello Alice, your score is 95
.RE
.PP
To print a pointer address:
.PP
.RS
.nf
int main(void)
{
    int x;
    _printf("Address: %p\\n", &x);
    return (0);
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Output (example):
.PP
.RS
Address: 0x7ffd57b0d4ac
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
This implementation does not support:
.IP \[bu] 2
Field width specification
.IP \[bu] 2
Precision modifiers
.IP \[bu] 2
Length modifiers (l, h)
.IP \[bu] 2
Flag characters (+, space, #, 0, \-)
.IP \[bu] 2
Floating point conversions
.IP \[bu] 2
Custom formatting beyond basic specifiers
